   boundaries.

3. Data taken from OpenStreetMap (from the Humanitarian OpenStreetMap Team's
   export of populated places on HDX, and from Overture Maps, which republishes
   OpenStreetMap places with their alternative names) is kept in separate files: town
   spellings in data/gazetteer/variants_osm.json, and villages, hamlets, towns
   and neighbourhoods in data/gazetteer/settlements_osm.json. Both are under
   the Open Database License (ODbL 1.0), with attribution "© OpenStreetMap
   contributors". The packages ship these files alongside the others; they are
   never merged into variants.json or settlements.json. The JavaScript build
   (dist/) embeds an unchanged copy of variants_osm.json, and so does anything
   built on that package; every record keeps its "osm:" source reference and
   stays under the ODbL.

     https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/odbl/

   What the ODbL does and does not cover:
   - It covers variants_osm.json and settlements_osm.json (and any copy or
     changed version of them). If you redistribute either file, or a database
     made from it, keep it under the ODbL,
     keep the attribution, and offer the changed database if you publish one.
   - It does not cover the other files. The boundaries, places.json,
     variants.json, settlements.json, the normalisation rules and the test
     cases are independent databases kept beside the OSM files, so the packages are a "Collective
     Database" (ODbL section 4.5) and those files keep their own licences
     (CC BY 3.0 IGO and CC BY 4.0).
   - Matching results (the P-codes and names returned for your data) are, at
     most, a "Produced Work" (ODbL section 4.3): they are not under the ODbL and
     need no share-alike. When a published result relies substantially on the
     OpenStreetMap spellings or villages, add "Contains information from OpenStreetMap,
